lesson(10,S,'genetics','mendels-monohybrid-cross',"Mendel and the monohybrid cross","The monk and the pea garden","Between 1856 and 1863 an Austrian monk grew about 10,000 pea plants in his monastery garden and counted the offspring of every cross. By counting carefully he found that heredity follows simple rules, and genetics was born.",
["Gregor Johann Mendel (1822-1884), an Austrian monk, discovered the basic principles of heredity through experiments on garden pea plants; he is the Father of Genetics. @ Mendel",
 "He worked from 1856 to 1863 on nearly 10,000 pea plants of 34 varieties and chose 7 pairs of contrasting characters, such as tall and dwarf plants. @ 7 pairs of characters",
 "Genetics is the branch of biology that deals with genes, genetic variation and heredity; heredity passes traits from one generation to the next, while variation is the difference among individuals. @ genetics",
 "A cross involving one pair of contrasting characters is a monohybrid cross, for example tall (T) with dwarf (t). @ monohybrid cross",
 "A pure tall plant crossed with a pure dwarf plant gave only tall plants in the F1 generation, so tall is dominant and dwarf is recessive. @ dominant and recessive",
 "Selfing the F1 plants gave tall and dwarf plants in the ratio 3:1 in the F2 generation. @ 3 : 1 ratio",
 "The genetic ratio in F2 is 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, so the phenotypic ratio is 3 tall : 1 dwarf. @ genotype and phenotype"],
("Cross two heterozygous tall plants (Tt x Tt). What fraction of offspring will be dwarf?",[("Each parent makes gametes T and t in equal numbers.","Gametes are T or t."),("Combining them gives TT, Tt, Tt and tt.","One in four are tt."),("Only tt plants are dwarf, so the fraction is 1/4 (and 3/4 are tall).","Phenotypic ratio 3:1.")]),
("State Mendel's monohybrid cross results and explain the terms dominant and recessive.",["F1 all tall","F2 tall:dwarf = 3:1","Dominant trait shows in F1","Recessive reappears in F2"],["Thinking the recessive trait is lost forever in F1"]),
["Mendel used garden pea plants and 7 pairs of contrasting characters.","Monohybrid cross: one pair of characters.","F1 all tall; F2 3 tall : 1 dwarf.","Genotype ratio 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt."],
["m|The Father of Genetics is|Gregor Johann Mendel|Charles Darwin~Proposed evolution by natural selection|Louis Pasteur~Worked on microbes|He used pea plants.",
 "n|Number of pairs of contrasting characters Mendel studied|7|Tall/dwarf is one pair.",
 "t|In Mendel's cross of pure tall and pure dwarf plants all F1 plants were tall.|True|Tall is dominant.",
 "m|The phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross is|3 : 1|1 : 1~Test cross ratio|9 : 3 : 3 : 1~Dihybrid ratio|3 tall : 1 dwarf.",
 "t|The dwarf trait disappears completely in the F1 generation and never returns.|False|It reappears in F2.",
 "s|A cross involving one pair of contrasting characters is a ____ cross.|monohybrid|Mono means one.",
 "n|If 1200 F2 plants are produced in the ratio 3:1, the expected number of dwarf plants|300|One quarter of 1200.",
 "m|Mendel worked on|garden pea plants|rats~Not used|fruit flies~Used by Morgan|Pisum sativum."],
svg=(punnett,"Punnett square for Tt x Tt","The F2 generation of a monohybrid cross"))
lesson(10,S,'nervous-system','neuron-stimulus-and-response',"The nervous system: neurons and coordination","Why you pull your hand from a hot pan","You touch something hot and your hand jumps away before you think. A stimulus was detected, an impulse travelled along nerves, and a response happened. The nervous system coordinates this in a fraction of a second.",
["A stimulus is a change in the environment detected by receptors; the body's reaction to it is the response, and working together to give a proper response is called coordination. @ stimulus and response",
 "Coordination between cells and organs keeps the body in physiological balance, called homeostasis. @ homeostasis",
 "The nervous system is made of nervous tissue with three components: neurons, neuroglia and nerve fibres. @ components",
 "A neuron or nerve cell is the structural and functional unit of the nervous system, specialised to detect, receive and transmit stimuli as electrical impulses. @ neuron",
 "A neuron has a cyton or cell body with a nucleus and Nissl's granules, dendrites that carry impulses towards the cyton, and an axon that carries impulses away. @ parts of a neuron",
 "Neuroglia are supporting glial cells that do not initiate or conduct impulses. @ neuroglia",
 "Neurons cannot divide, and nerve fibres are long slender processes bundled into nerves that connect receptors, the brain and effectors. @ nerve fibres"],
("You touch a hot vessel and pull your hand back. Trace the path from stimulus to response.",[("Heat is the stimulus; receptors in the skin detect it.","Stimulus and receptor."),("A sensory neuron carries the impulse to the spinal cord or brain.","Impulse travels inward."),("A motor neuron carries the command to the muscle, and the hand withdraws.","Response by the effector.")]),
("Define stimulus, response and coordination, and draw/describe a neuron.",["Stimulus is a change detected by receptors","Response is the reaction","Neuron: cyton, dendrites, axon"],["Mixing up dendrites and axon direction"]),
["Stimulus: change detected by receptors; response: reaction.","Nervous tissue: neurons, neuroglia, nerve fibres.","Neuron = structural and functional unit; has cyton, dendrites, axon.","Neurons carry electrical impulses and cannot divide."],
["m|The structural and functional unit of the nervous system is the|neuron|neuroglia~Supporting cells|nerve fibre~Part of a neuron|It carries impulses.",
 "t|Neuroglia conduct nerve impulses.|False|They only support neurons.",
 "m|The cell body of a neuron is called|cyton|dendrite~Receives impulses|axon~Carries impulses away|It has the nucleus and Nissl's granules.",
 "t|Dendrites carry impulses towards the cell body.|True|They are branched processes.",
 "s|A change in the environment detected by receptors is called a ____.|stimulus|Light, heat and sound are examples.",
 "t|Neurons have a high ability to divide.|False|Neurons cannot divide.",
 "m|Working together of organs to give a proper response is|coordination|digestion~Not that|excretion~Not that|It maintains homeostasis.",
 "m|Information moves in a neuron as|electrical impulses|sound waves~No|light~No|Neurons conduct impulses."])
